rnández-Cruz ∗\nL66: \nL67: Facultad de Ciencias, Universidad Nacional Autónoma de Mexico, Ciudad Universitaria, Av. Universidad 3000, Ciudad de\nL68: Mexico, 04510, CDMX, Mexico\nL69: \nL70: a r t i c l e i n f o\nL71: \nL72: Article history:\nL73: \nL74: Received 20 June 2024\nL75: \nL76: Received in revised form 23 November 2025\nL77: \nL78: Accepted 30 December 2025\nL79: \nL80: Available online 8 January 2026\nL81: \nL82: Keywords:\nL83: \nL84: Split graph\nL85: \nL86: Pseudo-split graph\nL87: \nL88: H-split graph\nL89: \nL90: Polar graph\nL91: \nL92: Degree sequence\nL93: \nL94: Linear-time algorithm\nL95: \nL96: a b s t r a c t\nL97: \nL98: A partition (A,B) of the vertex set of a graph G is called a polar partition of G when G[A]\nL99: \nL100: and G[B] are complete multipartite graphs. If (A,B) is a polar partition of G in which G[A]\nL101: and G[B] have at most s and k parts, respectively, then (A,B) is an (s,k)-polar partition\nL102: \nL103: of G, and G is said to be an (s,k)-polar graph. A graph is said to be unipolar (monopolar)\nL104: \nL105: if its vertex set admits a polar partition (A,B) such that A is a clique (an independent set,\nL106: \nL107: respectively). A graph admitting a (1,1)-polar partition is usually called a split graph.\nL108: \nL109: Naturally, most problems related to polar partitions are trivial on split graphs, even\nL110: when some of them are very hard in general. In this work, we present some results\nL111: \nL112: related to polar partitions on two graph classes generalizing split graphs. Our main\nL113: \nL114: results include efficient algorithms to decide whether graphs in these classes admit\nL115: \nL116: such partitions. We also establish upper bounds on the order of minimal (s,k)-polar\nL117: \nL118: obstructions for these families, for any s and k (possibly s= ∞ or k= ∞).\nL119: \nL120: © 2026 The Authors. Published by Elsevier B.V. This is an open access article under the CC\nL121: BY-NC-ND license (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/).\nL122: \nL123: 1. Introduction\nL124: \nL125: All graphs in this work are finite and simple. In general, we follow [1], although some notation may differ slightly.\nL126: \nL127: As usual, we use K n to denote the complete graph on n vertices, and H≤G to denote that the graph H is an induced\nL128: \nL129: subgraph of the graph G. We denote by G+H and G⊕H the disjoint union and the join of the graphs G and H, respectively.\nL130: Congruently, we use nG to denote the disjoint union of n copies of a graph G. Two subsets U and W of the vertex set of\nL131: \nL132: a graph G=(V,E) are said to be complete (respectively, anticomplete) if u w∈E (respectively, if u w /∈E) for every pair\nL133: \nL134: of distinct vertices u∈U and w∈W. For a family F of graphs, we say that a graph G is F-free if G contains no graph\nL135: \nL136: in F as an induced subgraph; if F= {F} we say that G is F-free instead of {F}-free. A property of graphs is said to be\nL137: hereditary if it is closed under taking induced subgraphs. A minimal P-obstruction for a hereditary property P of graphs\nL138: \nL139: is a graph that does not have the property P, but such that every vertex-deleted subgraph does.\nL140: \nL141: Given two nonnegative integers, s and k, a partition (A,B) of the vertex set of a graph G is called an (s,k)-polar partition\nL142: \nL143: of G if A induces a complete multipartite graph with at most s parts (i.e. a complete s-partite graph) and B induces the\nL144: disjoint union of at most k complete graphs (which is called a k-cluster); an (s,k)-polar graph is a graph admitting an (s,k)-\nL145: \nL146: polar partition. If either s or k (or both) are set to ∞, it means that there is no restriction on the number of parts of G[A],\nL147: \nL148: G[B], or both, respectively. A polar partition is an (∞,∞)-polar partition. A monopolar partition (respectively, a unipolar\nL149: \nL150: partition) is a polar partition (A,B) such that A is an independent set (respectively, a clique); notice that a monopolar\nL151: partition is exactly a (1,∞)-polar partition. Naturally, graphs admitting polar, monopolar, or unipolar partitions are called\nL152: \nL153: I The authors gratefully acknowledge support from SEP-CONACYT A1-S-8397 and UNAM-PAPIIT IN106425 grants.\nL154: \nL155: ∗Corresponding author.\nL156: \nL157: E-mail addresses: esteban.contreras@ciencias.unam.mx (F.E. Contreras-Mendoza), chc@ciencias.unam.mx (C. Hernández-Cruz).\nL158: \nL159: https://doi.org/10.1016/j.dam.2025.12.065\nL160: 0166-218X/© 2026 The Authors. Published by Elsevier B.V. This is an open access article under the CC BY-NC-ND license\nL161: \nL162: (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/).\nL163: \nL164: F.E. Contreras-Mendoza and C. Hernández-Cruz Discrete Applied Mathematics 384 (2026) 229–242\nL165: \nL166: Table 1\nL167: \nL168: P and NP-c indicate that the complexity status of the corresponding\nL169: \nL170: problem is polynomial or NP-complete, respectively.\nL171: \nL172: Graph class Monopolarity Polarity\nL173: \nL174: general graphs NP-c [6] NP-c [2]\nL175: \nL176: chordal O(|V|+|E|) [4] P [4]\nL177: P 5-free O(|V|4) [11] NP-c [4]\nL178: \nL179: {2 K 2,C 5}-free O(|V|4) [4] NP-c [4]\nL180: \nL181: hole-free P [4] NP-c [4]\nL182: \nL183: {C 4,C 5}-free planar NP-c [4] NP-c [4]\nL184: \nL185: polar, monopolar and unipolar graphs, respectively. The (1,1)-polar graphs are usually called split graphs. Two well-known\nL186: \nL187: characterizations of split graphs are given in the following proposition.\nL188: \nL189: Theorem 1 ([9,10]). Let G be a graph with vertex set {v 1, v 2, . . . , v n} and degree sequence d 1≥d 2≥ · · · ≥ d n, where d i is\nL190: the degree of vertex v i. Set p=max{i:d i≥i−1}. The following conditions are equivalent:\nL191: \nL192: 1. G is a split graph;\nL193: \nL194: 2. G is a {2 K 2,C 4,C 5}-free graph;\nL195: \nL196: 3. p\nL197: \nL198: i=1 d i=p(p−1) +n\nL199: \nL200: i=p+1 d i.\nL201: \nL202: Additionally, if G is a split graph, then ({v 1, . . . , v p},{v p+1, . . . , v n}) is a split partition of G.\nL203: \nL204: Note that, once the degree sequence of a graph G is known, computing the value of p, as well as verifying the condition\nL205: in item 3 of Theorem 1, can be done in O(|V|) time, so split graphs are recognizable and a split partition can be found in\nL206: \nL207: linear time from their degree sequences.\nL208: \nL209: It is known that, for any pair of nonnegative integers s and k, there are finitely many minimal (s,k)-polar obstruc-\nL210: \nL211: tions [8], and (s,k)-polar graphs can be recognized in O(|V|4+2max{s,k}) time [7]. Despite this, complete lists of minimal\nL212: (s,k)-polar obstructions are known only for the cases min{s,k} = 0 and s=k=1. It is well known that, for an integer\nL213: \nL214: k≥2, the (0,k)-polar graphs (k-clusters) are precisely the {P 3,(k+1)K 1}-free graphs, and the (0,∞)-polar graphs, called\nL215: \nL216: clusters, are the P 3-free graphs. Analogously, if s≥2, the (s,0)-polar graphs (complete s-partite graphs) coincide with the\nL217: \nL218: {P 3,K s+1}-free graphs, and the (∞,0)-polar graphs, better known as complete multipartite graphs, are exactly the P 3-free\nL219: \nL220: graphs.\nL221: Unipolar graphs have been shown to be recognizable in O(n 3) time [3,5] but, in contrast, the problems of deciding\nL222: \nL223: whether a graph is polar or monopolar are known to be NP-complete problems [2,6].\nL224: \nL225: Clearly, split graphs are unipolar, monopolar, and (s,k)-polar graphs for any positive integers s and k, so polarity is\nL226: \nL227: not quite interesting when restricted to split graphs. Nevertheless, some difficult problems related to polarity have been\nL228: studied in many different graph classes, including families of graphs related to split graphs for which such problems\nL229: \nL230: remain hard to solve. Some examples can be found in Table 1.\nL231: \nL232: Considering the results mentioned above and with the purpose of finding families of graphs that contain the class\nL233: \nL234: of split graphs but for which polarity problems can be efficiently solved, we study two generalizations of split graphs\nL235: \nL236: introduced by Maffray and Preissmann [12].\nL237: Given a fixed graph H, a graph G is said to be H-split if V G admits a partition (C,S,I) such that C is a clique, I is an\nL238: \nL239: independent set, either S=∅ or G[S]∼\nL240: \nL241: =H, C is complete to S, and I is anticomplete to S. A partition (C,S,I) as described\nL242: \nL243: above is called an H-split partition of G. If G=(C,S,I) is an H-split graph with S= ∅, we say that G is a strict H-split\nL244: \nL245: graph. Given a family of graphs H, we say that G is H-split if it is H-split for some H∈H. A sequence of nonnegative\nL246: integers is said to be uniquely realizable if, up to isomorphisms, there is only one graph having such a sequence as its\nL247: \nL248: degree sequence. The next theorem implies that, for a graph H whose degree sequence is uniquely realizable, the class of\nL249: \nL250: H-split graphs is recognizable in O(|V|) time and an H-split partition also can be found in O(|V|) time from their degree\nL251: \nL252: sequences.\nL253: \nL254: Theorem 2 ([12]). Let d∗\nL255: \nL256: 1≥ · · · ≥ d∗\nL257: \nL258: h be a uniquely realizable degree sequence and let H be its only realization. Let G be a\nL259: graph with degree sequence d 1≥ · · · ≥ d n, and let q be the maximum of the set {i:d i≥i−1+h} if it is not empty, or q=0\nL260: \nL261: otherwise. Then, G is an H-split graph if and only if G is a split graph or\nL262: \nL263: q\nL264: \nL265: \nL266: \nL267: i=1\nL268: \nL269: d i=q(q−1) +qh +\nL270: \nL271: n\nL272: \nL273: \nL274: \nL275: j=q+h+1\nL276: \nL277: d i\nL278: \nL279: and d q+i=q+d∗\nL280: \nL281: i for each i∈ {1, . . . , h}. Additionally, if the condition on the degrees holds, then the sets C= {v 1, . . . , v q},\nL282: \nL283: S= {v q+1, . . . , v q+h} and I= {v q+h+1, . . . , v n} form an H-partition of G.\nL284: It is a straightforward observation that, for a fixed graph H, the class of H-split graphs is self-complementary if and\nL285: \nL286: only if H is, and it is hereditary if and only if either H is a split graph, in which case H-split graphs coincide with split\nL287: \nL288: 230\nL289: \nL290: F.E. Contreras-Mendoza and C. Hernández-Cruz Discrete Applied Mathematics 384 (2026) 229–242\nL291: \nL292: graphs, or H∈ {2 K 2,C 4,C 5}. Additionally, if H is 2 K 2, C 4, or C 5, and G=(C,S,I) is a strict H-split graph, then the only\nL293: induced copy of H in G is G[S] and the H-split partition of G is unique.\nL294: \nL295: From the above observations, it is not strange that the most studied H-split graphs are the C 5-split graphs which were\nL296: \nL297: named pseudo-split graphs in [12], where it was also proved that they are precisely the {2 K 2,C 4}-free graphs. Naturally,\nL298: \nL299: a C 5-split partition of a graph is also called a pseudo-split partition and, since pseudo-split graphs are strict if and only if\nL300: they are not perfect, strict pseudo-split graphs are called imperfect.\nL301: \nL302: In line with this work, we study monopolar, unipolar, and (s,k)-polar partitions on H-split graphs whenever H∈\nL303: \nL304: {C 5,2 K 2,C 4}. The rest of the paper is organized as follows. Section 2 is devoted to polarity on pseudo-split graphs; we\nL305: \nL306: provide characterizations for pseudo-split (s,k)-polar graphs, from which we derive linear-time recognition algorithms\nL307: depending only on the degree sequence of the input graph. We also present complete lists of pseudo-split minimal (1,k)-\nL308: \nL309: and (2,k)-polar obstructions for fixed arbitrary nonnegative integer k, and we show tight upper bounds (linear in s+k) for\nL310: \nL311: the order of pseudo-split minimal (s,k)-polar obstructions. In particular, we show that there are finitely many pseudo-split\nL312: \nL313: minimal (s,∞)- and (∞,k)-polar obstructions. Section 3 deals with results concerning polarity in 2 K 2-split graphs that\nL314: are analogous to those given in Section 2 for pseudo-split graphs. In contrast with the results presented in Section 2, we\nL315: \nL316: prove that 2 K 2-split graphs admitting an (s,k)-polar partition cannot always be recognized by their degree sequence, but\nL317: \nL318: they still can be recognized in linear time. Also, some of the upper bounds that we present for the order of 2 K 2-split\nL319: \nL320: minimal (s,k)-polar obstructions are not linear in s+k, and we do not know whether they are tight. Since C 4-split graphs\nL321: are the complements of 2 K 2-split graphs, analogous results are deduced for these graphs. Finally, in Section 4 we propose\nL322: \nL323: some open problems and conjectures.\nL324: \nL325: 2. Polarity on pseudo-split graphs\nL326: \nL327: Split graphs are trivially polar, monopolar, unipolar, and (s,k)-polar graphs for any positive integers s and k. Similarly,\nL328: \nL329: if G=(C,S,I) is an imperfect pseudo-split graph and S′ is a maximum clique of G[S], then a polar partition of G is\nL330: given by (C∪(S\\S′),I∪S′); hence, pseudo-split graphs are polar graphs. In this section we study polarity properties on\nL331: \nL332: pseudo-split graphs. As our main results, we provide O(|V|)-time algorithms to decide whether a pseudo-split graph is\nL333: \nL334: (s,k)-polar from its degree sequence, even when one of s or k is ∞, and we prove tight upper bounds for the order of\nL335: \nL336: pseudo-split minimal (s,k)-polar obstructions.\nL337: It is worth noticing that C 5 admits only two essentially different polar partitions (A,B). The first is a (2,1)-polar partition\nL338: \nL339: with A inducing P 3 and B inducing K 2; the second is a (1,2)-polar partition with A inducing K 2 and B inducing P 3. By\nL340: \nL341: using the previous observation, in the next proposition we summarize some facts about the general structure of any\nL342: \nL343: polar partition of an arbitrary imperfect pseudo-split graph.\nL344: Lemma 3. Let G=(C,S,I) be an imperfect pseudo-split graph, and let (A,B) be a polar partition of G. Let M C be the set of\nL345: \nL346: vertices in C that are anticomplete to I, and let M I be the set of vertices in I that are complete to C.\nL347: \nL348: 1. If G[S] inherits a (2,1)-polar partition from (A,B), then I⊆B. Otherwise, if G[S] inherits a (1,2)-polar partition from\nL349: \nL350: (A,B), then C⊆A.\nL351: \nL352: 2. If C∩B= ∅, then G[S] inherits a (2,1)-polar partition from (A,B). Similarly, if I∩A= ∅, then G[S] inherits a (1,2)-polar\nL353: partition from (A,B)\nL354: \nL355: 3. C\\M C⊆A and I\\M I⊆B.\nL356: \nL357: Proof.\nL358: \nL359: 1. If (A∩S,B∩S) is a (2,1)-polar partition of G[S], then, since I is anticomplete to S and A induces a P 3-free graph,\nL360: \nL361: we have that I⊆B. The other implication can be proved similarly.\nL362: \nL363: 2. Let u∈C∩B. Since G[B] is a cluster, B induces a P 3-free graph, so B∩S is a clique and A∩S induces P 3. Therefore,\nL364: \nL365: G[S] inherits a (2,1)-polar partition from (A,B). The other implication admits an analogous proof.\nL366: 3. Let u∈C\\M C, and let v∈I∩N(u). If u/∈A, then u∈B, so C∩B= ∅ and we have from items 1 and 2 that v∈B.\nL367: \nL368: But then, for any w∈B∩S, {u, v, w} induces P 3, which is impossible because B induces a cluster. Therefore, u∈A,\nL369: \nL370: so we conclude that C\\M C⊆A. That I\\M I⊆B can be proved analogously. □\nL371: \nL372: 2.1. Recognition of pseudo-split (s,k)-polar graphs\nL373: \nL374: Lemma 3 reveals that the sets M C and M I are the key to determine polar partitions of an imperfect pseudo-split graph.\nL375: In this section, we use such a property to completely characterize pseudo-split graphs admitting unipolar, monopolar,\nL376: \nL377: and (s,k)-polar partitions. From these characterizations we derive efficient algorithms to recognize pseudo-split graphs\nL378: \nL379: having the mentioned partitions.\nL380: \nL381: We begin by pointing out a direct consequence of Theorem 2. Notice that, in the notation of Lemma 3, this observation\nL382: implies that, for any pseudo-split graph G=(C,S,I), the cardinalities of C, I, M C, and M I, can be computed in O(|V|) time\nL383: \nL384: from the degree sequence of G.\nL385: \nL386: 231\nL387: \nL388: F.E. Contreras-Mendoza and C. Hernández-Cruz Discrete Applied Mathematics 384 (2026) 229–242\nL389: \nL390: Remark 4. Let G=(C,S,I) be an imperfect pseudo-split graph. For each u∈V G, d(u)= |C| + 4 if and only if u is a vertex\nL391: \nL392: in C that is anticomplete to I, and d(u)= |C| if and only if u is a vertex in I that is complete to C.\nL393: It can be easily verified that C 5 is a minimal unipolar obstruction and, since split graphs are unipolar, it follows that\nL394: \nL395: a pseudo-split graph is unipolar if and only if it is a split graph, which can be checked in O(|V|) time from its degree\nL396: \nL397: sequence. Note that a perfect pseudo-split graph is a split graph and hence an (s,k)-polar graph for any fixed positive\nL398: \nL399: integers s and k. Now, we present a necessary and sufficient condition for a pseudo-split graph to be (s,k)-polar for any\nL400: fixed nonnegative integers s and k with s+k≥1.\nL401: \nL402: Theorem 5. Let G=(C,S,I) be an imperfect pseudo-split graph, and let s and k be nonnegative integers. Let M C be the set\nL403: \nL404: of vertices in C that are anticomplete to I, and M I be the set of vertices in I that are complete to C . Then, G is an (s,k)-polar\nL405: \nL406: graph if and only if either\nL407: \nL408: 1. k ≥ |I| + 1 and s≥ |C|−|M C| + 2, or\nL409: \nL410: 2. s ≥ |C| + 1 and k≥ |I|−|M I| + 2.\nL411: Proof. Let c= |C|,i= |I|,m c= |M C|, and m i= |M I|. First, let us suppose that G admits an (s,k)-polar partition (A,B). If\nL412: \nL413: G[S] inherits a (2,1)-polar partition from (A,B), we have from Lemma 3 that I⊆B and C∩B⊆M C. Thus,\nL414: \nL415: |C∩A| = |C|−|C∩B| ≥ c−m C,\nL416: \nL417: where we conclude that s≥c−m C+2. Furthermore, in this case G[B] is a cluster with exactly i+1 components, so\nL418: \nL419: k≥i+1. Hence, we have proved that, if G[S] inherits a (2,1)-polar partition from (A,B), then k≥i+1 and s≥c−m C+2.\nL420: If G[S] does not inherit a (2,1)-polar partition form (A,B), then it inherits a (1,2)-polar partition, and it can be proved\nL421: \nL422: using analogous arguments that in such a case s≥c+1 and k≥i−m I+2.\nL423: \nL424: Conversely, let us assume that k≥i+1 and s≥c−m C+2. Let B 1 be a maximum clique of G[S], and let A 1=S\\B 1.\nL425: \nL426: Then, we have that (A 1∪(C\\M C),B 1∪M C∪I) is a (c−m C+2,i+1)-polar partition of G, so G is an (s,k)-polar graph, as\nL427: we had to prove. The result follows analogously if we assume that s≥c+1 and k≥i−m I+2, only taking a (1,2)-polar\nL428: \nL429: partition (A 1,B 1) of G[S] instead of a (2,1)-polar partition. □\nL430: \nL431: Notice that, by Remark 4, the conditions in Theorem 5 can be verified in O(|V|) time from the degree sequence of the\nL432: \nL433: input graph, so (s,k)-polarity can be decided in linear time on this family of graphs, and an (s,k)-polar partition can be\nL434: \nL435: found with the same time complexity.\nL436: Next, we characterize pseudo-split graphs admitting an (s,∞)-polar partition, where s is any fixed nonnegative integer.\nL437: \nL438: Once again, it follows from Remark 4 that the conditions in Theorem 6 can be verified in O(|V|) time from the degree\nL439: \nL440: sequence of any pseudo-split graph, so (s,∞)-polarity can be decided in linear time on pseudo-split graphs, and an (s,∞)-\nL441: \nL442: polar partition can be found with the same complexity. Since monopolar graphs are precisely the (1,∞)-polar graphs,\nL443: the same is true for monopolarity on pseudo-split graphs.\nL444: \nL445: Theorem 6. Let s be a nonnegative integer. If G=(C,S,I) is an imperfect pseudo-split graph, then G is an (s,∞)-polar graph\nL446: \nL447: if and only if either s≥ |C| + 1, or |C| ≥ s≥2 and there are at least |C| − s+2 vertices in C that are anticomplete to I.\nL448: \nL449: Proof. Let us denote |C| by c. Suppose that (A,B) is an (s,∞)-polar partition of G such that s≤c. Observe that, by Lemma\nL450: 3, if the restriction of (A,B) to S is a (1,2)-polar partition, then C⊆A, but that is impossible since G[A] would have K c+1\nL451: \nL452: as an induced subgraph and s≤c. Thus, G[S] is covered by a (2,1)-polar partition, and we have from Lemma 3 that I⊆B.\nL453: \nL454: Since A induces a complete s-partite graph and G[A∩S] is a complete bipartite graph, at most s−2 vertices of C belong\nL455: \nL456: to A. Then, at least c−s+2 vertices of C belong to B, and we have from Lemma 3 that any such vertex is anticomplete\nL457: to I. This proves that, if G is (s,∞)-polar, then it satisfies the stated conditions on c and s.\nL458: \nL459: For the converse implication, let A and B be a maximum independent set and a maximum clique of G[S], respectively.\nL460: \nL461: If c<s, then (C∪A,I∪(S\\A)) is an (s,∞)-polar partition of G. Otherwise, we have that c≥s and the subset C′ of C of\nL462: \nL463: all vertices that are anticomplete to I has at least c−s+2 elements. Then, ((S\\B)∪(C\\C′),I∪C′∪B) is an (s,∞)-polar\nL464: \nL465: partition of G. □\nL466: Since pseudo-split graphs form a self-complementary class of graphs, and a graph G is (s,∞)-polar if and only if G\nL467: \nL468: is (∞,s)-polar, we have, by a simple argument of complements, that a characterization analogous to Theorem 6 can be\nL469: \nL470: easily stated for (∞,k)-polarity on pseudo-split graphs.\nL471: \nL472: Now, we present some results about pseudo-split minimal (s,k)-polar obstructions, which include tight upper bounds\nL473: for the order of such graphs, as well as complete lists of minimal forbidden induced subgraphs for some small values of\nL474: \nL475: s and k.\nL476: \nL477: 2.2. Pseudo-split minimal (s,k)-polar obstructions\nL478: \nL479: As we mentioned before, minimal (s,k)-polar obstructions on general graphs are known only for the cases min{s,k} =\nL480: \nL481: 0, and s=k=1, which correspond to clusters, complete multipartite graphs, and split graphs. In the following proposition\nL482: \nL483: 232\nL484: F.E. Contreras-Mendoza and C. Hernández-Cruz Discrete Applied Mathematics 384 (2026) 229–242\nL485: \nL486: we give complete lists of pseudo-split minimal (s,k)-polar obstructions for the case s∈ {1,2}, which can be easily\nL487: \nL488: extrapolated to the case k∈ {1,2} by a simple complementation argument. Before presenting such results, we introduce\nL489: \nL490: some particular graphs.\nL491: \nL492: Let G 0\nL493: \nL494: s=(C,S,I) be the imperfect pseudo-split graph such that |C| = s, |I| = 1, and C is complete to I. Let G 1\nL495: \nL496: s be the\nL497: \nL498: graph obtained from G 0\nL499: s by deleting one edge incident with the only vertex of I. By using Theorem 5, it is easy to verify\nL500: \nL501: that G 0\nL502: \nL503: s and G 1\nL504: \nL505: s are minimal (s,k)-polar obstructions whenever s and k are integers, s,k≥2.\nL506: \nL507: Proposition 7. Let k be an integer, k≥2. If G=(C,S,I) is a pseudo-split graph, then\nL508: \nL509: 1. G is a (1,k)-polar graph if and only if G is a K 1⊕C 5-free graph.\n"
